WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.900
This video describes normal and abnormal fundal red reflex appearance.

2
00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:19.370
A normal fundal red reflex examination is when there is no overall difference in the brightness and colour between the two eyes with the central corneal reflections.

3
00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:31.530
Even a normal appearance of the fundal reflex will, however, vary depending on how far away you are and where the child is looking.

4
00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:42.010
It is an active dynamic examination where you should move side to side and back and forth in and around arm's length from the patient.

5
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:51.350
Babies and children will also move their hands and eyes, making the reflex change the way it looks during the examination.

6
00:00:51.910 --> 00:01:02.350
In a black baby, the reflex typically looks quite pale, with yellow and white and sometimes even blue as the main colour.

7
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:14.970
Importantly, in a normal examination, there should be no overall difference in the brightness and colour between the two eyes with the central corneal reflections.

8
00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:23.850
An abnormal reflex is when there is a persistent, real difference in the brightness and colour between the two eyes.

9
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:35.840
A displaced corneal reflection, a dark patch present within the reflex of one eye or both eyes, or a completely absent red reflex.

10
00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:56.330
An abnormal reflex test can indicate the presence of cataract, retinoblastoma, corneal scar, squint, refractive error or congenital problems such as coloboma.

11
00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:01.690
These all require further assessment by a specialist.

12
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:10.900
Look at this reflex and ask yourself: is this normal or abnormal? The answers will follow afterwards.

13
00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:45.840
You should now have a better understanding of what a normal reflex looks like, as well as abnormal reflexes, and be able to suggest the most likely cause.

